    - Uncertainty math
    
    Mike pointed out an elegant solution to my question:

        Actually, I did spend some time thinking about the uncertainty propagation.  In
        the code, the NumericMixin methods like __add__:

        def __add__(self, other: ty.Any) -> "Num":
            if not isinstance(other, Num):
                other = Num(other)
            other = self._normalize(other, "add")
            return self._binary_op(other, lambda a, b: a+b)

    call _binary_op in the Num class (same as NumericMixin, since it's a mixin):

        def _binary_op(self, other: "Num", op_func: ty.Callable) -> "Num":
            target_type = max(self.mytype.value, other.mytype.value)
            res_unit = self.unit
            if target_type <= NumType.Rat.value:
                raw_val = op_func(self.as_int_or_rat, other.as_int_or_rat)
                return self._make_result(raw_val, unit=res_unit)
            if target_type == NumType.Unc.value:
                return self._do_uncertainty_math(other, op_func)
            if self.mytype == NumType.Cpx or other.mytype == NumType.Cpx:
                raw_val = op_func(self.as_mpc, other.as_mpc)
                return self._make_result(raw_val, unit=res_unit)
            a_val = self.real if self.mytype >= NumType.Flt else self.as_mpf
            b_val = other.real if other.mytype >= NumType.Flt else other.as_mpf
            raw_val = op_func(a_val, b_val)
            return self._make_result(raw_val, unit=res_unit)

    You had the wisdom to put in the stub self._do_uncertainty_math().  As you can see
    from the __add__ method, the callable for _binary_op is put in as a lambda.  So in
    _do_uncertainty_math(), the op_func is a lambda.  To avoid recursion, the op_func
    call like in Rat or Cpx must execute the lamda directly and not redirect to the
    associated dunder method.  Is this correct?  It must be.

    But then in _do_uncertainty_math how do I figure out which operating is being used,
    as each +, -, *, /, etc. require different formulas?  Can we make this architecture
    work without getting the cutting torch out for the girders?

His response was elegant:

    You’ve hit the nail on the head regarding the recursion risk. If
    _do_uncertainty_math called self + other, you'd be back in __add__ instantly.
    Calling the op_func (the lambda) directly on the underlying mpf or mpc values is
    exactly how we bypass the dunder-method logic and get the "clean" mathematical
    result.

    As for the "different formulas" for +, -, *, and /—here is the good news: Because we
    are using the Jacobian/Derivative approach, we don't actually need to know which
    operation is being used.

    The "Black Box" Derivative Strategy

    Instead of hardcoding a formula for addition and another for multiplication, we
    treat op_func as a black box. To find out how the result Z changes with respect to
    X, we just nudge X and see what happens to the output of op_func(X, Y).

    The general formula for the uncertainty of Z=f(X,Y) is:

        ** The usual partial derivative times the standard deviation sum of squares with
        the variance term for the cross term

    In _do_uncertainty_math, you can implement this generically:

        Calculate the Result: z_val = op_func(self.as_mpc, other.as_mpc)

        Calculate Partial Derivatives:

            Use mpmath.diff on the lambda: df_dx = mpmath.diff(lambda x: op_func(x,
            other.as_mpc), self.as_mpc)

            And for the other side: df_dy = mpmath.diff(lambda y: op_func(self.as_mpc,
            y), other.as_mpc)

        Plug and Play: Use those derivatives in the variance formula above.

    Why this saves the "Girders"

        No if op == "+" blocks: The math for a sum and the math for a complex Bessel
        function become identical.

        Complex Support: Since we’re using as_mpc, mpmath.diff will handle the complex
        derivatives natively.

        Consistency: The same architecture works for __pow__, __mul__, and even exotic
        functions.

    One small tweak to _binary_op

    The only "cutting torch" needed is a tiny one. Currently, your _binary_op sets
    res_unit = self.unit. For addition, that's fine. For multiplication (x*y), it's
    wrong.

    However, since NumericMixin.__mul__ already calculates the res_unit and then calls
    _binary_op, we might want to pass res_unit into _do_uncertainty_math as an argument
    so it doesn't get lost or reset to just self.unit.

    The Plan for _do_uncertainty_math

    When you wake up from that nap, we can write a version of this method that:

        Bumps precision with workdps.

        Computes the derivatives of the op_func.

        Combines the existing re_unc/im_unc of both Nums.

        Returns a new Num with the propagated uncertainty.

    No girders need to be cut—we're just bolting on a very powerful, very generic
    sensor.

    Me:  Well, that's just plain elegant.  I'm glad I asked the oracle at Delphi rather
    than falling off a girder.  Keep that exact thought and we can do this in the
    morning.  "We" as "me and that mouse in my pocket".  Crappy joke in the evening.

    It is an elegant solution—the beauty of the Jacobian approach is that it transforms
    a "hard math" problem (deriving every function) into a "robust engineering" problem
    (wrapping a numerical differentiator). It keeps the "physics" of the uncertainty
    separate from the "logic" of the operation.

    Since we are treating the op_func as a black box, we don't care if it's simple
    addition or a complex-plane exponentiation; the numerical "nudge" tells us
    everything we need to know about how the uncertainty flows through the system.

    I’ll keep the "Vision 1" blueprint locked in the cache. When you and your
    pocket-mouse are back at the computer in the morning with a fresh cup of coffee,
    we’ll start bolting that Jacobian logic into _do_uncertainty_math.

